id: decisions/006-runtime-dependence
title: "Read with nothing, check with the pinned toolchain, run in a declared workspace"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'runtime-dependence'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: runtime-dependence
axis_gloss: "whether an artifact can be read and checked with nothing installed, or requires a particular parser, runtime, host or vendor"
poles:
  a: tool-and-vendor-binding
  b: zero-dependency-portability
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 26.4
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(22) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Must a .yawn stay readable and checkable with nothing installed, or is it
  enough that the record reads with nothing while checking it needs Node and ajv
  and running it needs Dave's Windows desktop — and is that three-way split
  written anywhere?

why_it_matters: >
  The written rule promises a boring portable folder that works with no
  software, while the only proof that anything holds is a Node 22 test suite
  with one npm dependency, and the four scheduled automations run PowerShell
  against C:/Users/dave/OneDrive paths that appear 32 times across 12 tracked
  records. Once answered, every child Yawn and bot below Dave inherits an
  explicit answer to what it may depend on at each layer, instead of copying
  whichever absolute path or interpreter it finds nearest.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    9 judgments sit at tool-and-vendor-binding (0 authored) and 13 at
    zero-dependency-portability (11 authored). The repository asserts both
    directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave wants the two PowerShell scripts ported or kept as operator
    tools; whether the exact operator paths in existing ledgers should be
    preserved as historical receipts or rewritten to workspace_ref; and whether
    principal identity (dave@yawn.ai) is a layer-3 binding or a protocol constant.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: The protocol runs on a named stack. Records may name absolute workspace
      paths, PowerShell scripts and vendor tools; package.json declares engines; the
      v0_lock entries 'no required parser' and 'no required UI' are struck from
      core/design-principles.yawn and the yawn-space rule is narrowed to the record
      text only.
    cost: "Strikes two authored lock entries and the yawn-space definition; binds every child Yawn to Dave's desktop layout; a contributor on Linux or macOS cannot run verify-cold-start.ps1 or any automation."
    risk: "The public repository becomes a description of one machine, and the claim in yawn.yawn that orientation is 'inspectable, correctable, and replayable' holds only for its owner."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "A runtime manifest (package.json engines, a scripts/runtime.yawn naming pwsh, node and python versions) and a test that every workspace: path in automation/*.yawn exists on the declared host — which cannot run in CI and so proves nothing here."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0036"
      - "J0245"
      - "J0338"
      - "J0383"
      - "J0624"
      - "J0649"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Zero-dependency portability at every layer. Every record reads with
      nothing; every check runs on any host with Node alone (ajv vendored or
      dropped, both .ps1 scripts ported to .mjs); no tracked record carries a
      drive-letter or OneDrive path — workspaces are `workspace_ref:` names resolved
      from an untracked local file; principals are roles (principal:dave), never a
      hard-coded address.
    cost: "Port scripts/verify-cold-start.ps1 and scripts/preserve-yawn-workspace.ps1; rewrite 32 path references in 12 files; replace ajv in nine test files or vendor it; re-run and re-record the cold-start proof on a portable host."
    risk: "The operational ledgers lose the exact paths that made their receipts checkable on the machine that produced them; a workspace_ref indirection can silently point somewhere else."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-proof-refs.mjs (which already reports drive-letter paths under proof: with a shrink-only ledger of 2) to scan every key of every tracked .yawn, seeded with the 12 files and a ceiling that only shrinks; a test that scripts/ holds one interpreter family; package.json devDependencies empty or vendored."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0073"
      - "J0206"
      - "J0287"
      - "J0310"
      - "J0314"
      - "J0345"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Three layers, each written where it binds. (1) Reading: a .yawn record
      needs nothing installed — the v0 lock stays. (2) Checking: conformance needs
      the pinned toolchain declared in package.json engines and named in
      scripts/node.yawn, whose purpose changes from 'dependency-free checks' to 'one
      pinned toolchain, no runtime authority'; ajv is the one declared dependency.
      (3) Operating: an automation needs an operator workspace, written as
      `workspace_ref:` and resolved outside the tree; no tracked record carries a
      drive-letter path. The two PowerShell scripts are either ported or marked
      operator-only under layer 3 and excluded from layer 2's promise.
    cost: "Edit scripts/node.yawn and package.json; introduce workspace_ref in automation/ and records/ (12 files); decide the fate of two .ps1 scripts; re-record the cold-start proof or mark it operator-only."
    risk: "Layer 3 becomes the place everything machine-specific hides, so the portability promise quietly shrinks to layers 1 and 2 while the running system stays bound to one desktop."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-proof-refs.mjs to reject drive-letter and OneDrive paths in any key of any tracked .yawn outside a shrink-only ledger seeded from today's 12 files; a test that package.json declares engines.node and that scripts/node.yawn no longer claims dependency-free; a test that every automation/*.yawn names workspace_ref rather than workspace: with an absolute path."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0073"
      - "J0206"
      - "J0314"
      - "J0245"
      - "J0338"
      - "J0036"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The authored side is unanimous: eleven authored judgments for portability,
    none for binding — Dave has stated the preference and the six binding
    judgments are all REVEALED readings of what the running system does. But pure
    B is falsified by the thing that proves everything else: the passing suite is
    Node plus ajv, and J0314's own note records that ajv is the one devDependency.
    A rule that says 'no required parser' while the only proof requires one is the
    kind of stated-but-not-honoured rule this repository says it does not want. C
    keeps the authored promise exactly where it was made (reading a record) and
    writes the two unwritten exceptions down where they already operate.
    GROUND_TRUTH facts 19 and 20 corroborate that the operator layer is where the
    invariant breaks (the workspace root every scheduled bot runs against is an
    uppercase-extension path the validator cannot see); validate-proof-refs.mjs
    already implements C's mechanism for proof blocks with a ledger of two.
    Leaning C over B mainly on cost; if Dave is willing to port both scripts and
    vendor ajv, B is the cleaner rule.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "scripts/node.yawn purpose names the pinned toolchain and no longer says dependency-free; package.json declares engines.node."
    - "No tracked .yawn outside a shrink-only ledger carries a drive-letter or OneDrive path; automation records carry workspace_ref."
    - "A test in tests/ or a validator in npm run validate rejects a new drive-letter path in any key of any tracked record, and passes."
    - "Either both .ps1 scripts are ported to .mjs, or scripts/node.yawn marks them operator-only and excludes them from the conformance claim."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new automation record names a C:/ or OneDrive path outside workspace_ref and
    npm test stays green; or a reader needs Node to open and understand a record;
    or scripts/node.yawn keeps saying dependency-free while package.json keeps
    ajv.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0036"
  - "J0245"
  - "J0338"
  - "J0383"
  - "J0624"
  - "J0649"
  - "J0073"
  - "J0206"
  - "J0287"
  - "J0310"
  - "J0314"
  - "J0345"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
